<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pearl River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zhujiang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pearl River is the great river system of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, formed by the West, North and East rivers, which meet in a broad delta at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> before reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>. In ancient times its western branches linked the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> with the lowlands of Nanyue. Its delta, which now includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>, has been a centre of maritime trade since antiquity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pearl-river.jpg" alt="Pearl River" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tung Wu from Shenzhen, China · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humen_Bridge-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
